[[File:Timeline of Gallup polls in US on legalizing marijuana.png|thumb|350px|Timeline of [[Gallup, Inc|Gallup polls]] in US on legalizing marijuana.<ref name=galluppolls>{{cite news |last1=Saad |first1=Lydia |title=Grassroots Support for Legalizing Marijuana Hits Record 70% |url=https://news.gallup.com/poll/514007/grassroots-support-legalizing-marijuana-hits-record.aspx |work=Gallup.com |publisher=[[Gallup, Inc.]] |date=8 November 2023 |language=en}} Data link is at bottom of graph.</ref>]]
[[Cannabis in the United States|In the United States]], [[Cannabis (drug)|cannabis]] is legal in 42 of [[U.S. state|50 states]] for medical use and 24 states for recreational use. At the federal level, cannabis is generally classified as a [[List of Schedule I controlled substances (U.S.)|Schedule&nbsp;I drug]] under the [[Controlled Substances Act]], determined to have a high potential for abuse.<ref name="mpp report">{{citation|title=State-By-State Medical Marijuana Laws|url=https://www.mpp.org/assets/pdf/issues/medical-marijuana/State-by-State-Laws-Report-2015.pdf|publisher=[[Marijuana Policy Project]]|date=December 2016}}</ref> However, licensed medical cannabis is classified as [[List of Schedule III controlled substances (U.S.)|Schedule III]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-04-23 |title=Office of Public Affairs {{!}} Justice Department Places FDA-Approved Marijuana Products and Products Containing Marijuana Subject to a Qualifying State-issued License in Schedule III, Strengthening Medical Research While Maintaining Strict Federal Controls {{!}} United States Department of Justice |url=https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-places-fda-approved-marijuana-products-and-products-containing-marijuana |access-date=2026-04-23 |website=www.justice.gov |language=en}}</ref> reflecting its medical use and lower abuse potential, while unlicensed cannabis products remain Schedule I substances. Despite these prohibitions, federal law is generally [[Unenforced law|not enforced]] against the possession, cultivation, or intrastate distribution of cannabis in states where such activity is legal under state law.<ref name=CRS>{{citation|title=The Federal Status of Marijuana and the Expanding Policy Gap with States|publisher=Congressional Research Service|date=March 6, 2023|url=https://crsreports.congress.gov/product/pdf/IF/IF12270|id=IF12270|quote=Although state laws do not affect the status of marijuana under federal law ... the federal response to states' legalizing or decriminalizing marijuana largely has been to allow states to implement their own laws...Federal law enforcement has generally focused its efforts on criminal networks involved in the illicit marijuana trade. }}</ref><ref name="NLR-AG-Garland">{{cite journal |last1=Haviland |first1=Jane |title=Attorney General Merrick Garland on DOJ's New Marijuana Policy |website=www.natlawreview.com |date=March 16, 2023 |volume=XIV |issue=1 |url=https://www.natlawreview.com/article/ag-garland-signals-department-justice-s-cannabis-policy-will-be-very-close-to-cole |access-date=2 January 2024 |language=en}}</ref> During the [[117th United States Congress]], a [[Marijuana Opportunity Reinvestment and Expungement Act|bill]] to decriminalize marijuana in federal law passed the [[United States House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] but failed in the [[United States Senate|Senate]]. In April 2024, during the Biden administration, the Department of Justice initiated a process to [[Removal of cannabis from Schedule I of the Controlled Substances Act|reschedule cannabis to the less-restrictive Schedule&nbsp;III]].<ref name="To be rescheduled">{{cite news |last1=Miller |first1=Zeke |last2=Goodman |first2=Joshua |last3=Mustian |first3=Jim |last4=Whitehurst |first4=Lindsay |title=US poised to ease restrictions on marijuana in historic shift, but it'll remain controlled substance |url=https://apnews.com/article/marijuana-biden-dea-criminal-justice-pot-f833a8dae6ceb31a8658a5d65832a3b8 |access-date=1 May 2024 |work=[[Associated Press News]] |date=May 1, 2024}}</ref> [[Executive Order 14370]], issued December 18, 2025, during the second Trump administration, expedited the process of rescheduling.<ref>{{cite news |author1=Maher |first=Kit |author2=Contorno |first2=Steve |author3=Owermohle |first3=Sarah |date=December 18, 2025 |title=Trump signs executive order expediting marijuana reclassification after lobbying from cannabis industry |url=https://www.cnn.com/2025/12/18/politics/trump-marijuana-reclassification-executive-order |publisher=CNN}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-12-18 |title=Increasing Medical Marijuana and Cannabidiol Research |url=https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/12/increasing-medical-marijuana-and-cannabidiol-research/ |website=The White House |language=en-US}}</ref>

The [[Medical cannabis in the United States|medical use of cannabis]] is legal with a medical recommendation in 42 states, four out of five permanently inhabited [[Territories of the United States|U.S. territories]],{{efn|Guam, Northern Mariana Islands, Puerto Rico, U.S. Virgin Islands}} and the federal [[Cannabis in Washington, D.C.|District of Columbia]] (D.C.).<ref name="NCSL MMJ">{{cite web|date=June 22, 2023|title=State Medical Cannabis Laws|url=https://www.ncsl.org/research/health/state-medical-marijuana-laws.aspx|access-date=April 26, 2024|publisher=[[National Conference of State Legislatures]]|archive-date=February 6, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210206034555/https://www.ncsl.org/research/health/state-medical-marijuana-laws.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref> Eight other states have laws that limit the [[psychoactive]] compound [[tetrahydrocannabinol]] (THC) concentration, for the purpose of allowing access to products rich in [[cannabidiol]] (CBD), a non-intoxicating component of cannabis.<ref name="NCSL-LT2">{{cite web |title=State Medical Cannabis Laws |url=https://www.ncsl.org/health/state-medical-cannabis-laws#table2 |website=www.ncsl.org |access-date=28 June 2026}}</ref><ref name="NCSL MMJ"/><ref name=MPP-CBD>{{cite web |title=Overview of States' Flawed High-CBD Laws |url=https://www.mpp.org/issues/medical-marijuana/overview-of-states-flawed-high-cbd-laws |website=[[Marijuana Policy Project]] |access-date=April 26, 2024}}</ref> The [[Rohrabacher–Farr amendment]], first passed in 2014, prohibits federal prosecution of individuals complying with state medical cannabis laws.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Ingraham|first1=Christopher|date=June 13, 2017|title=Jeff Sessions personally asked Congress to let him prosecute medical-marijuana providers|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/wonk/wp/2017/06/13/jeff-sessions-personally-asked-congress-to-let-him-prosecute-medical-marijuana-providers/|url-access=limited|access-date=December 31, 2017}}</ref>

The [[Legalization of non-medical cannabis in the United States|recreational use of cannabis]] has been legalized in 24 states, three U.S. territories, and D.C.{{efn|States that have legalized the use of recreational marijuana, including laws which have not yet gone into effect: Alaska, Arizona, California, Colorado, Connecticut, Delaware, Illinois, Maine, Maryland, Massachusetts, Michigan, Minnesota, Missouri, Montana, Nevada, New Jersey, New Mexico, New York, Ohio, Oregon, Rhode Island, Vermont, Virginia, and Washington<br /><br />Territories that have legalized recreational marijuana include: Guam, Northern Mariana Islands, U.S. Virgin Islands}} Another seven states have decriminalized its use.{{efn|Hawaii, Louisiana, Mississippi, Nebraska, New Hampshire, North Carolina, North Dakota}}<ref name="NCSL decrim">{{cite web|date=October 17, 2019|title=Marijuana Overview|url=https://www.ncsl.org/research/civil-and-criminal-justice/marijuana-overview.aspx|access-date=December 31, 2017|publisher=[[National Conference of State Legislatures]]|archive-date=April 17, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190417182433/http://www.ncsl.org/research/civil-and-criminal-justice/marijuana-overview.aspx|url-status=dead}}</ref> Commercial distribution has been legalized in all jurisdictions where possession has been legalized, except D.C. Personal cultivation for recreational use is allowed in all of these jurisdictions except for [[Cannabis in Delaware|Delaware]], [[Cannabis in Illinois|Illinois]], [[Cannabis in New Jersey|New Jersey]], and [[Cannabis in Washington (state)|Washington state]].

[[Cannabinoid]] drugs which have received approval from the [[Food and Drug Administration]] (FDA) for prescription use are [[Dronabinol|Marinol and Syndros]] (synthetic THC is the active ingredient in both), Cesamet ([[nabilone]]), and Epidiolex (CBD). For non-prescription use, products with less than 0.3% delta-9 THC containing CBD, [[Delta-8-Tetrahydrocannabinol|delta-8 THC]], and other naturally occurring cannabinoids derived from [[hemp]] (cannabis containing less than 0.3% delta-9 THC) are legal and unregulated<ref name=McPhillips.CNN>{{Cite news |last=McPhillips |first=Deidre |date=January 26, 2023 |title=FDA indicates need to better regulate CBD products and plans to work with Congress on new rules |url=https://www.cnn.com/2023/01/26/health/cbd-new-regulation-fda/index.html |access-date=June 26, 2023 |publisher=[[CNN]] |language=en}}</ref> at the federal level, but legality and enforcement varies by state.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Hudak |first1=John |date=December 14, 2018 |title=The Farm Bill, hemp legalization and the status of CBD: An explainer |url=https://www.brookings.edu/blog/fixgov/2018/12/14/the-farm-bill-hemp-and-cbd-explainer/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200225163555/https://www.brookings.edu/blog/fixgov/2018/12/14/the-farm-bill-hemp-and-cbd-explainer/ |archive-date=February 25, 2020 |access-date=June 3, 2019 |publisher=[[The Brookings Institution]]}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last1=Williams |first1=Timothy |date=May 6, 2019 |title=CBD Is Wildly Popular. Disputes Over Its Legality Are a Growing Source of Tension. |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/06/us/cbd-cannabis-marijuana-hemp.html |url-status=live |url-access=limited |access-date=June 3, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190530091654/https://www.nytimes.com/2019/05/06/us/cbd-cannabis-marijuana-hemp.html |archive-date=May 30, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Mentzer |first=Rob |date=January 4, 2022 |title=A loophole in federal marijuana law has led to the creation of new THC product |publisher=[[NPR]] |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/01/04/1070338052/a-loophole-in-federal-marijuana-law-has-led-to-the-creation-of-new-thc-product}}</ref>

Statewide legalization of recreational cannabis appeared on the ballot for the [[2024 United States elections|2024 elections]] as [[2024 Florida Amendment 3]], and in North and South Dakota. It failed to pass in all 3 states.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://norml.org/blog/2024/08/12/north-dakota-marijuana-legalization-measure-will-appear-on-november-ballot/|title=North Dakota: Marijuana Legalization Measure Will Appear on November Ballot|publisher=[[NORML]]|date=August 12, 2024|access-date=August 22, 2024}}</ref> Nebraskans decided on a ballot measure related to medical cannabis the same day, which passed.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://norml.org/blog/2024/09/03/nebraska-voters-to-decide-on-medical-cannabis-access-measures//|title=Nebraska Voters To Decide on Medical Cannabis Access Measures|publisher=[[NORML]]|date=September 3, 2024|access-date=September 8, 2024}}</ref>
